WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.220
Why did none of Mendel's counts match his ratio?

2
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:10.620
He crossed peas that differed in seed shape and seed colour.

3
00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.020
Then he counted the seeds from the hybrids.

4
00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:18.713
His ratio predicts how the seeds should split into four kinds.

5
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:22.473
Not one of his four piles matched its prediction.

6
00:00:23.773 --> 00:00:24.853
Was the ratio wrong?

7
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:27.267
Start with one seed.

8
00:00:28.527 --> 00:00:30.707
It began as an egg and a pollen grain.

9
00:00:32.047 --> 00:00:34.927
Each carried one version of each gene, picked by chance.

10
00:00:36.307 --> 00:00:38.847
So every seed is a fresh draw from the same odds.

11
00:00:40.187 --> 00:00:43.347
For seed shape alone, round comes up three times in four.

12
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.700
Now add seed colour as a second fork.

13
00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.100
To be round and yellow, a seed must take both branches.

14
00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.600
So follow the path, and multiply the chances along it.

15
00:00:56.533 --> 00:00:58.913
Some questions count more than one path.

16
00:01:00.173 --> 00:01:05.193
A seed with exactly one recessive trait could be round and green, or wrinkled and

17
00:01:05.193 --> 00:01:09.133
yellow. No seed can be both, so add those paths.

18
00:01:11.617 --> 00:01:14.637
So the word 'and' means multiply along a path.

19
00:01:15.717 --> 00:01:17.537
The word 'or' means add the paths.

20
00:01:18.697 --> 00:01:20.617
Those two rules build the whole ratio.

21
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.790
But a ratio gives chances, not exact counts.

22
00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:30.550
Apply it to Mendel's total, and the round yellow pile comes out with part of a seed.

23
00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:33.850
You cannot grow three quarters of a seed.

24
00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:37.230
So a ratio cannot give the exact pile.

25
00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:39.973
So let chance grow the cross.

26
00:01:41.193 --> 00:01:46.033
A computer replays it with the same number of seeds, and the ratio exactly true.

27
00:01:47.313 --> 00:01:49.373
How far from the lines will the runs land?

28
00:01:55.417 --> 00:02:00.657
No run can sit exactly on a line, since the lines fall at fractions of a seed.

29
00:02:01.977 --> 00:02:06.537
Each run lands a little high in one class and a little low in another, by its own

30
00:02:06.537 --> 00:02:09.937
amounts. Now drop in Mendel's real counts.

31
00:02:11.177 --> 00:02:12.577
They sit among the chance runs.

32
00:02:13.937 --> 00:02:16.237
His misses are well within what chance makes.

33
00:02:17.317 --> 00:02:19.877
This works only when the steps are independent.

34
00:02:21.377 --> 00:02:24.357
Genes on different chromosomes pass on independently.

35
00:02:25.897 --> 00:02:29.937
Genes far apart on one chromosome behave almost as if unlinked.

36
00:02:31.337 --> 00:02:34.197
Genes close together tend to be inherited together.

37
00:02:35.397 --> 00:02:37.657
Then you cannot simply multiply their chances.

38
00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:43.060
Here is the idea. A cross predicts probabilities.

39
00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.560
Multiply along a path, and add the paths that count.

40
00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.520
And a probability predicts a spread of counts, not one exact count.

41
00:02:53.583 --> 00:02:56.343
So the real question is not whether counts match.

42
00:02:57.563 --> 00:03:01.763
It is how far a count can stray before chance becomes a poor explanation.

43
00:03:03.123 --> 00:03:04.363
That is chapter 2.
